  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a multifunctional nanoparticle designed for drug delivery or similar biomedical use. It shows a roughly spherical particle in cross-section, with a central hollow or solid core that can be silica, metallic, or magnetic, surrounded by a porous shell. The shell is depicted as a siloxane or silsesquioxane framework with tunable pore size, emphasizing that the interior can be engineered to hold molecular cargo.\n\nInside the pores and inner cavity, various cargos are illustrated as small molecules or particles, suggesting encapsulation and transport of therapeutic or diagnostic agents. Around the outside, several layers and surface features are added to modify how the particle behaves in biological environments. One side highlights a supported lipid bilayer, while another shows a polymer coating and PEG chains, indicating strategies to improve stability, circulation, or biocompatibility. Targeting ligands are attached to the surface to direct the particle toward specific cells or tissues.\n\nThe outermost pore openings appear capped with supramolecular nanovalves, implying controlled release. Overall, the image conveys a modular nanocarrier whose core, porous framework, coatings, and surface chemistry can all be tailored for targeted and regulated delivery.",
